        "columns": [
          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "When we put life at the centre of governance, what changes in our social practices?",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In a world marked by the climate crisis and social inequalities, the concept of social biomimicry and bioregionalism is emerging as a revolutionary proposal for rethinking local governance. Òscar Gussinyer talks with Melina Ángel, an expert in biomimicry applied to social systems and a member of the Meraki organisation in Colombia; together they explore how indigenous communities and ecosystems can inspire models of governance that put life at the centre. ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "&nbsp;",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "title": null,
            "titleHeading": null,
