          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>Listening to the Territories, Listening to Life</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "According to Melina, one of the keys to sustainable governance is learning to listen deeply to territories. In Colombia, indigenous communities maintain a direct relationship with the land that goes beyond an extractive or utilitarian vision. “For these communities, the land is not a resource; it is a living system with which we must dialogue and live in harmony,” she says.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This philosophy is reflected in their practices: before building a bridge or a path, they consult the ecosystems and perform rituals of connection. This shows a way of acting that prioritises sustainability and respect for nature, creating a model that could be adopted by other local governments.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>The Challenge of Decolonising Governance</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Another key aspect of Melina's reflection is the recognition of how colonialism has shaped the way we manage territories. In Europe, for example, colonial history has also left its mark on government structures. Small communities, such as rural municipalities, often reproduce models of centralisation that do not respond to the real needs of the territory.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Melina suggests a fundamental question to change this dynamic: “How can we put life at the centre?” This perspective involves understanding what is alive in a territory, whether social needs, cultural tensions or ecological processes. Only through this kind of listening can authentic and inclusive responses emerge.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>Harmony between Scales: From Local to Global</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "One of the challenges Melina highlights is the obsession with scaling up solutions. Nature teaches us that complexity emerges from local interactions: individuals, communities and their relationships. Just as a forest works through the harmony between trees, plants and animals, human communities also need dynamics of listening and cooperation in order to flourish.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "“Governance is an act of holding what is invisible: connections, shared needs, relationships,” says Melina. These processes cannot be imposed from above; they must arise from the dynamics of each territory.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "bold": true,
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>Bioregions: A New Way of Belonging</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Bioregionalism offers a new way of understanding territories. Rather than relying on arbitrary political borders, it recognises the natural boundaries defined by geography, ecosystems and local cultures. “Bioregions define themselves. They are spaces where communities feel they belong, where there is an intrinsic relationship with the land,” Melina explains.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This deep connection with the territory not only satisfies vital needs, but also encourages reciprocity and regeneration. For example, rivers can be seen not as simple resources, but as part of a living system that needs care to regenerate and benefit the entire community.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>An Economy Based on Life</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Finally, Melina points out that we need to rethink the economy. Instead of relying on extraction and export, we can take inspiration from nature, where flows of energy, resources and information are organised to generate local abundance. This does not mean isolation, but sustainable relationships with other bioregions.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "“A forest is an example of a living economy: it accumulates resources not to exploit them, but to sustain its entire web of life. This is the model we should adopt,” she concludes.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "<h4>Biomimicry: A Path Open to Everyone</h4>",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Melina stresses that you do not need to be a biologist or have specialised academic training to apply biomimicry. Life itself teaches us. “The key is to listen: to listen to nature, to listen to ourselves and to listen to others. Life is within us and all around us. We just need to open ourselves to this connection.”",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This approach reminds us that we are part of the biosphere, not separate agents. If we learn to act with this awareness, we will be able to build local governance that is fairer, more harmonious and aligned with the processes of life.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
