          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Bioregionalism and biomimicry offer us an opportunity to rethink local governance from a more harmonious perspective, connected with nature. Melina Ángel reminds us that putting life at the centre of decision-making is key to creating models that are sustainable and inclusive, respecting both human needs and the dynamics of ecosystems. This transformation involves not only listening to territories but also unlearning the structures inherited from colonialism, in order to move towards a more equitable relationship with the land and between communities.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
